WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.660
- [Dr. Zarka] Deep within
the Amazon rainforest

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.010
a mysterious creature lurks.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.320
Notoriously elusive,

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.080
this cave-dwelling giant hairy monster

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.970
leaves a trail of broken trees

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.240
and trampled ferns in its wake.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.890
With no predators to speak of,

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.410
the Mapinguari's fearsome
strength is matched

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:18.920
by its pungent odor.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.800
Appearing predominantly in the traditions

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.300
of the peoples of Brazil,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.240
the Mapinguari seeks vengeance
against those who hunt

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.170
and harvest the flora
and fauna of its home,

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.530
those it deems violators

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.480
of the sacred spaces of the rainforest.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.270
The Amazon claims
unparalleled biodiversity

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:36.103
on our globe,

00:00:36.103 --> 00:00:39.050
and demands a heightened
level of protection.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.320
So is the Mapinguari real, mythical,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.953
or somewhere in between?

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.660
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.473
And this is Monstrum.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.730
Of massive size, great strength,

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:54.400
and possessing a distinctive foul smell,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.930
the hairy creature roars

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.470
with great ferocity from its
shortened horse-like muzzle.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.430
The Mapinguari may have two eyes

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.230
or only one in the center of its forehead,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.540
and a mouth in the center of its stomach.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.640
It usually roams the
rainforest on all fours,

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.750
but eyewitnesses also claim
that it can walk bipedally.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.440
When standing on its hind legs,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.180
the Mapinguari can reach anywhere

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.940
between three to six meters in height.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.580
Adorning all four giant anteater-like paws

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.360
are large hooked claws like
those of a giant armadillo,

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.960
that curl inwards or backward.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.670
It also has some pretty
impressive chompers,

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.950
including four huge canine
teeth protruding from its mouth.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.180
Color variations range from
reddish, to brown, to black,

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.400
and it has a short tail.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.410
While the Mapinguari
prefers a vegetarian diet,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.330
it will feast on livestock

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.900
and humans that violate its habitat.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.700
It kills by ripping the legs

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.810
and arms off its prey before eating them.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.480
For the particularly bad
offenders that pillage the forest

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.010
with greed, in the most brutal accounts

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.580
the creature devours the victim slowly,

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.930
starting with the head
and proceeding leisurely.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.250
The hard skin of the
Mapinguari may resemble

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.010
the skin of an alligator
or a turtle's shell.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:10.510
But regardless of appearance,

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:13.490
it's virtually impenetrable to weapons.

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:16.010
Another weak point, it's belly button.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.620
If you get the chance to
try to kill the monster,

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:19.770
depending on the source,

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.360
it's horrific odor or
supernatural abilities

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.540
disorient would-be hunters.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.730
Those that might get a
lucky shot off may not

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.760
even be able to get near
enough to verify the kill

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.340
because the smell emanating
from the remains is so foul,

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.430
it cannot be tolerated by even those

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.220
with the strongest stomachs.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.900
The first tales of the Mapinguari come

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.620
from the indigenous peoples
of the Amazon basin,

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.550
where legends of forest creatures abound.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.713
In the Amazon, across the
states of Rondonia, Amazonas,

00:02:47.713 --> 00:02:51.280
Acre and Para, the Mapinguari
is part of folklore

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.070
and reported in eyewitness accounts alike.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.750
Brazil is home to more than
about 300 indigenous groups

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.030
with great linguistic diversity.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.060
With upwards of 150 languages
across so many communities,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.740
it's typical to find variations

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.773
in this monster's name or story.

00:03:29.216 --> 00:03:31.020
- That's Dr. Andriolli Costa,

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.020
folklore researcher and consultant.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.290
He's an expert in Brazilian folklore,

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.380
and a great storyteller to boot.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.710
- Other monsters share characteristics

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.680
with the Mapinguari, like the Segamai,

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.750
an herbivore about the size
of a cow, with matted fur,

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.820
who can walk on all fours
or it's back two legs,

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.320
emits a blood curdling roar,

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.970
and cannot be harmed by regular weapons,

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.410
but has a snout more like
that of a giant anteater.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.623
Then there's the protective Curupira.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.280
- It's tempting to use the similarities

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.890
of the Mapinguari myth
across regional traditions

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.200
to connect the creature to an animal.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.250
One that lived not so long ago

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.890
or even one still alive today.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.560
This is where the most popular

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.490
and notorious rationalization

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.670
for the Mapinguari comes into play.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.080
The giant sloth theory,
which claims the Mapinguari

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.580
and other similar creatures
are folkloric interpretations

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.400
of a real but long-extinct animal.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.650
Though, in the spirit of
other elusive monsters,

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.760
there are some people who chase legends

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:07.010
to prove their existence.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.380
In the 19th century,
written claims emerged

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.530
from South America,
proclaiming that members

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:14.110
of the Mylodontidae ground sloth family,

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:17.580
believed to be extinct, were
actually alive and well.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.070
Argentinian paleontologist
Florentino Ameghino

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.810
publicly asserted he
believed giant ground sloths

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.330
still lived in the Patagonia region.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:27.850
Even in the 21st century,

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:29.980
the "Mapinguari is a Ground Sloth theory"

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.550
is enthusiastically supported.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.830
Perhaps no more publicly than biologist

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.910
and ornithologist Dr. David Oren.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:40.040
Oren has dedicated a
large portion of his life

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.900
to proving the continued existence

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.880
of prehistoric ground sloths.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.110
He asserts that the monster is the result

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.900
of cultural memories combined

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.980
with present day living
ground sloth encounters,

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:54.400
most likely a relative of
the Mylodontidae family.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.060
Some of the hunters Oren talked

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.960
to said they had even killed members

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:01.870
of this subset of the Xenarthan
clade species themselves.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.410
On one of Oren's excursions,

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:05.160
a dung sample thought to be evidence

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.090
of the creature was collected.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.240
Using DNA analysis, a
lab proved the true owner

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:13.290
of the scat, an arboreal anteater.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:16.050
Science doesn't necessarily explain all

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.460
of the firsthand accounts

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.583
or hundreds of years of narratives.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.750
- Like the European
colonizers and travelers

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.100
who once influenced these stories,

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:52.580
Western audiences and internet
folklore sleuths today

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:56.060
have blurred the lines between
these supernatural monsters

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.230
and their indigenous symbolism.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.840
While not necessarily
done with ill intent,

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.380
it's important to keep in mind

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.450
that the original Mapinguari tales hold

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:06.113
the greatest significance.

00:09:48.012 --> 00:09:51.570
- If we take a close look at
all of the Mapinguari stories

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.320
from various indigenous
communities in Brazil

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.400
there is a consistent theme.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.100
The creature's purpose is
to protect the rainforest.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:02.850
For thousands of years,
the abundance of riches

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.180
within this natural resource has sustained

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:06.920
the people of the region.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:08.680
The existence of a scary monster

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.240
set out to protect it makes sense.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.210
The Mapinguari avenges its home

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.880
from trespassers, hunters
and greedy consumers alike.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.060
And if the Mapinguari's fearsome presence

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.400
can help save this significant resource,

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:23.883
another monster has served its purpose.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.527
Mylodontidae.

00:10:27.527 --> 00:10:28.850
Tidae?

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:29.730
Mylodontidae.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:30.563
Oh yeah, duh.

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:31.453
Mylodontidae.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.900
Good enough.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:34.733
Yeah.

